    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2026-1337",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Reconstructing spatial climate variability from proxy records requires forward models \u201cemulators\u201d that capture the dynamical structure of the climate system while remaining computationally efficient. Traditional emulators based on Empirical Orthogonal Functions and Linear Inverse Models (LIMs) face inherent limitations due to linearity and variance-based dimensionality reduction. Here we develop and evaluate a hierarchy of CMIP-class climate model emulators that integrate autoencoder-based dimensionality reduction with nonlinear prediction architectures, including Reservoir Computing (RC) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs). Using a comprehensive experimental protocol applied to the IPSL-CM6A-LR model and 52 CMIP6s models, we show that combining autoencoder and RC (AERCn) provides the most robust performance across time scales and dynamical regimes when data are plentiful. The AERCn configuration captures nonlinear features of ENSO and Atlantic Multidecadal Variability, maintains high spatial reconstruction skill, and generalizes across distinct climate model structures. When training data are scarce, a multimodel pre-trained AERNN provides a data-efficient and competitive alternative. These properties make the proposed architectures particularly well suited for integration into Particle Filters and Ensemble Kalman Filter PDA frameworks. Our results highlight the importance of predictability-oriented dimensionality reduction and nonlinear dynamical memory for emulator design, and they provide a scalable path toward improved reconstructions of climate variability over the Common Era.",
    "field": "Paleoclimate model emulation",
    "problem": "Traditional climate emulators for paleoclimate reconstruction, based on Linear Inverse Models and Empirical Orthogonal Functions, are limited by linearity and variance-based dimensionality reduction, hindering their ability to efficiently capture complex climate dynamics and memory effects necessary for accurate reconstructions.",
    "method": "The authors propose new climate model emulators that combine autoencoder-based dimensionality reduction with nonlinear prediction architectures, like Reservoir Computing and Recurrent Neural Networks, to improve the skill of paleoclimate reconstructions by better capturing complex climate dynamics.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and evaluate a hierarchy of climate model emulators, integrating autoencoder-based dimensionality reduction with nonlinear prediction architectures, to improve reconstructions of past climate variability.",
